<mujoco model="pen_pickplace_scene">
  <!--
    Coordinate convention: world origin (0,0,0) = center of the table's
    short (80cm) edge, at tabletop height. +X runs along the table's 160cm
    length (away from the arm, into the task space); +Y runs across its
    80cm width. Tabletop surface is z=0 (so the arm sits flush on it); the
    table extends down to a floor at z=-1.2 (matching the plan's 120cm
    table height).

    The arm's own mount point is NOT at this origin -- it's offset to
    (0, 0.15, 0) via base_link's `pos` in assets/so101/so101.xml (see
    ARM_MOUNT_POS in scripts/fetch_so101_urdf.py for why it lives there and
    not as a wrapper element here), moved 15cm along the short edge, away
    from the front camera, for more separation between the arm and the
    camera in its own view. All other positions in this file (table, can
    sampling bounds, bin) are still plain world coordinates, unaffected by
    that offset -- only the arm's reach envelope shifts with it.

    Phase 0 measured the arm's real horizontal reach at 44.0cm (see
    assets/so101/so101.xml generation report) -- notably more than
    PLAN.md's rough 30-35cm guess, which it explicitly flagged as
    unverified. The can and bin below are placed within that measured
    44cm radius, not the original guess.

    PEN-VS-CAN DECISION (Phase 2 trigger test): the original pen + pen-box
    design scored 5% (1/20) on the scripted grasp-trigger test, well under
    PLAN.md's 80% threshold. Root cause (not a controller bug): SO-101's
    gripper is a single hook-style finger closing against a fixed palm
    right at gripper_frame_link -- for a pen lying flush on the table, the
    palm's own bulk reaches the pen's position before the moving jaw can
    close on it, just pushing the pen out of the way during descent. This
    matches PLAN.md's own upfront risk framing (thin objects are much less
    forgiving for this gripper than a can). Per PLAN.md's decision rule,
    switched to the can+bin fallback below, reusing
    lerobot/examples/tutorial/gym_hil_desk_cleanup/desk_cleanup_env.py's
    two-geom can pattern -- a standing can gives the palm clearance under it
    that a flush-lying pen never could. Dimensions later shrunk from that
    verbatim reuse (CAN_RADIUS=0.03, CAN_HALF_HEIGHT=0.06), first to a
    small/mini cola can (0.026/0.045), then to CAN_RADIUS=0.022,
    CAN_HALF_HEIGHT=0.038 (~44mm diameter x ~76mm tall), and finally
    thinned further to CAN_RADIUS=0.019 (~38mm diameter, height
    unchanged) rather than a standard 330ml can. Bin footprint is shrunk from
    desk_cleanup's own (half-size 0.09, sized for the Franka's much larger
    workspace) down to half-size 0.06, to fit SO-101's smaller 44cm reach.
  -->
  <include file="../so101/so101.xml"/>

  <asset>
    <texture type="skybox" builtin="gradient" rgb1="0.65 0.75 0.85" rgb2="0.15 0.15 0.2" width="256" height="256"/>
    <texture name="floor_tex" type="2d" builtin="checker" rgb1="0.22 0.22 0.22" rgb2="0.27 0.27 0.27" width="300" height="300"/>
    <material name="floor_mat" texture="floor_tex" texrepeat="10 10" reflectance="0.0"/>

    <!-- Realistic can mesh: OpenRobotics's official "Coke Can" Gazebo Fuel
         model, converted from the earlier so101_sim2real Gazebo project's
         fetched .dae (Collada -- MuJoCo can't load that format directly)
         via trimesh, since this Blender install (5.2 LTS) dropped Collada
         import entirely. Re-centered so the body origin sits at the
         object's own geometric center (matching this scene's convention:
         a body's freejoint pos = the object's center height above the
         table) and exported true-to-scale in meters (real can: 67mm dia x
         124mm tall -- the DAE's own <unit meter="0.00254" name="inches"/>
         tag is misleading; the raw coordinates already matched real
         millimeters almost exactly, not inches, verified by checking
         against a real can's known size before trusting it). `scale`
         below shrinks it to this scene's chosen small-can size (38mm dia x
         76mm tall -- xy shrunk again from an earlier 44mm-dia pass to make
         the can thinner; z left alone since only the diameter changed) --
         see assets/objects/coke_can/ for the source conversion notes.
         MuJoCo does NOT auto-import the OBJ's own MTL/texture reference
         (verified empirically: ntex/nmat stayed 0 until declared
         explicitly here). -->
    <mesh name="coke_can_mesh" file="../objects/coke_can/coke_can.obj" scale="0.567079 0.567079 0.613398"/>
    <texture name="coke_can_tex" type="2d" file="../objects/coke_can/material_0.png"/>
    <material name="coke_can_mat" texture="coke_can_tex"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <light pos="0.3 0 1.6" dir="0 0 -1" diffuse="0.7 0.7 0.7" directional="false"/>
    <light pos="0.2 -0.6 0.9" dir="0.15 0.5 -0.7" diffuse="0.4 0.4 0.4" directional="false"/>

    <geom name="floor" type="plane" size="3 3 0.1" pos="0 0 -1.2" material="floor_mat"/>

    <!-- table top: 160cm (x) x 80cm (y), 3cm thick, top surface flush at z=0 -->
    <geom name="table_top" type="box" pos="0.8 0 -0.015" size="0.8 0.4 0.015" rgba="0.92 0.92 0.9 1"/>

    <!-- 4 legs, inset 5cm from each corner, tabletop underside down to the floor -->
    <geom name="table_leg_1" type="cylinder" pos="0.05 0.35 -0.615" size="0.03 0.6" rgba="0.5 0.5 0.5 1"/>
    <geom name="table_leg_2" type="cylinder" pos="0.05 -0.35 -0.615" size="0.03 0.6" rgba="0.5 0.5 0.5 1"/>
    <geom name="table_leg_3" type="cylinder" pos="1.55 0.35 -0.615" size="0.03 0.6" rgba="0.5 0.5 0.5 1"/>
    <geom name="table_leg_4" type="cylinder" pos="1.55 -0.35 -0.615" size="0.03 0.6" rgba="0.5 0.5 0.5 1"/>

    <!-- fixed front camera: elevated, angled down at the near reachable zone,
         to avoid the arm's own body occluding the workspace. quat is a
         look-at rotation targeting (0.30,-0.02,0.02) -- SAME x as the
         camera's own pos -- computed via a look-at matrix -> mju_mat2Quat.
         An earlier version targeted a point with a different x (0.27 vs pos
         x=0.30): technically zero-roll (verified: local "right" axis was
         exactly horizontal), but that slight sideways yaw made the table's
         real edges (straight lines parallel to world X) recede at an angle,
         so ordinary perspective convergence made them look tilted even
         though the camera itself wasn't rolled. Keeping target x == pos x
         makes the viewing direction perpendicular to those edges, so they
         render level. `pos` sits 10cm further out than the original
         (0.30,-0.40,0.40), along the SAME eye-to-target ray, so the quat
         (viewing angle) didn't need to change -- only distance did.
         Regenerate the quat the same way if the target or viewing angle
         ever changes, keeping pos's x equal to the target's x. -->
    <camera name="front" pos="0.30 -0.47071068 0.47071068" quat="0.92387953 0.38268343 0 0"/>

    <!-- can: realistic Coca-Cola mesh for looks, primitive cylinder for
         physics -- decoupling visual detail from collision geometry, the
         standard fix for exactly the problem hit here: the real scanned
         mesh's base isn't a perfectly flat disk, so MuJoCo's convex-hull
         collision on the raw mesh kept toggling between slightly different
         contact points at rest, a small but persistent jitter (~0.001-0.003
         m/s, ~0.02-0.07 rad/s, verified never decaying to the ~0.001 at-rest
         baseline the plain primitive cylinder settled to). The visual mesh
         geom is contype/conaffinity=0 (never touched by the physics engine
         at all) and carries negligible mass; the invisible primitive
         cylinder underneath is what the gripper and table actually contact,
         identical to the earlier primitive-only can's proven-stable
         geometry. ~24cm from the arm's mount point. Free joint for
         per-episode pose randomization (Phase 2). -->
    <body name="can" pos="0.22 -0.10 0.038">
      <freejoint/>
      <geom name="can_visual" type="mesh" mesh="coke_can_mesh" material="coke_can_mat" mass="0.0001"
            contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>
      <!-- solref stiffened 4x from MuJoCo's default (0.02 1) -- verified via an
           isolated pusher-vs-can test (scratch/test_contact_softness.py) that
           for a brief, moderate overlap (the kind a careful teleop approach
           that slightly overshoots would cause), this settles the can's
           post-contact velocity back out roughly 10x faster (final speed
           ~0.005 vs ~0.03-0.04 m/s). Does NOT fully prevent instability if
           the gripper is driven persistently through the can for a long
           span (a targeting/depth-judgment issue, not a contact-softness
           one -- verified separately that stiffening barely changes that
           case) -- SO-101 has no force sensing, so avoiding that is a
           piloting skill (watch the camera feeds), on real hardware too. -->
      <!-- radius shrunk 0.022 -> 0.019 (thinner can); mass scaled down with it
           (0.0088 -> 0.0066, proportional to r^2 at constant height) to keep
           the same density rather than silently making the can artificially
           denser when it got thinner. -->
      <geom name="can_collision" type="cylinder" size="0.019 0.038" mass="0.0066" solref="0.005 1"
            contype="1" conaffinity="1" rgba="0 0 0 0"/>
    </body>

    <!-- bin: open-top primitive container (bottom + 4 walls), ~34cm from
         the arm's mount point, fixed to the table. Same floor+4-wall
         construction as the old pen box (and desk_cleanup's basket), just
         resized to comfortably hold the can. -->
    <body name="bin" pos="0.30 0.15 0">
      <geom name="bin_bottom" type="box" pos="0 0 0.005" size="0.06 0.06 0.005" rgba="0.7 0.4 0.15 1"/>
      <geom name="bin_wall_pos_x" type="box" pos="0.06 0 0.03" size="0.005 0.06 0.03" rgba="0.7 0.4 0.15 1"/>
      <geom name="bin_wall_neg_x" type="box" pos="-0.06 0 0.03" size="0.005 0.06 0.03" rgba="0.7 0.4 0.15 1"/>
      <geom name="bin_wall_pos_y" type="box" pos="0 0.06 0.03" size="0.06 0.005 0.03" rgba="0.7 0.4 0.15 1"/>
      <geom name="bin_wall_neg_y" type="box" pos="0 -0.06 0.03" size="0.06 0.005 0.03" rgba="0.7 0.4 0.15 1"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <!--
    "home" keyframe: the first 6 qpos values are the arm's hand-set
    HOME_QPOS (from scripts/fetch_so101_urdf.py) -- NOT this model's qpos0,
    which is all-zeros for the arm since no joint sets `ref` (an earlier,
    reverted design tried baking HOME_QPOS in via `ref`, which turned out to
    shift what qpos geometrically MEANS rather than just setting a start
    state -- see so101.xml generation notes). The remaining 7 values (the
    can's freejoint) DO come verbatim from `model.qpos0` after loading this
    file, since the can's rest pos/quat has no such `ref` mechanism to
    conflict with. ctrl matches the arm's HOME_QPOS so the position
    actuators hold still there instead of driving back toward ctrl=0.

    This is deliberately defined HERE, not in assets/so101/so101.xml, and
    deliberately sized for this scene's full 13 qpos / 6 ctrl -- an earlier
    version put a 6-value keyframe in the bare arm asset, and when that got
    <include>d here, MuJoCo silently padded the missing 7 slots (the
    manipulated object's freejoint) with ZEROS rather than qpos0's real
    values. That specific padded keyframe never actually got applied in
    practice (confirmed by reading mujoco.viewer's source: it never touches
    keyframes when loading a model by path), but IS a live landmine for
    anything that does apply it by name (Phase 2's env reset(), or the
    GUI's own keyframe selector), so it's still fixed here regardless.

    Actually opening this scene: don't use `python -m mujoco.viewer
    --mjcf=...` -- it always starts from a plain, keyframe-blind
    mujoco.MjData(model), which for this model means qpos AND ctrl both
    start at zero (arm folded, not the home pose), and the position
    actuators immediately start driving hard toward that zero-ctrl target --
    visible as the arm shaking and swinging away from its spawn pose the
    instant the viewer opens. Use scripts/view_scene.py instead, which
    resets to this keyframe (setting qpos and ctrl to match) before
    launching.

    If you change HOME_QPOS, or the can's default pos/quat in this file,
    this keyframe goes stale silently (no error, just a wrong reset pose
    again) -- regenerate the can's 7 values by loading this scene and
    printing model.qpos0[6:13] (the arm's first 6 values are HOME_QPOS,
    unaffected by qpos0).
  -->
  <keyframe>
    <key name="home" qpos="0 -1.75 1.62 1.18 0 0 0.22 -0.1 0.038 1 0 0 0" ctrl="0 -1.75 1.62 1.18 0 0"/>
  </keyframe>
</mujoco>
